    {
      "say": "In 1914 two chemists in Manchester, Edward Ardern and William Lockett, blew air through sewage for weeks. A brown sludge formed, and when they kept it and added fresh sewage, it cleaned the water in hours. They called it activated sludge, and most cities still use it. On the board, the whole loop.",
